 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law and the education law, in relation to\nthe definition of person in parental relationship to a child",
    "basePrintNo": "S4475",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4475-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 19,
       "fullName": "Roxanne J. Persaud",
       "imgName": "736_Roxanne_Persaud.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 914,
       "sessionMemberId": 1207,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "PERSAUD"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §2164, Pub Health L; amd §3212, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4475\n\nSPONSOR: MONTGOMERY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the public health law and the educa-\ntion law, in relation to the definition of person in parental relation-\nship to a child\n\n \nPURPOSE: OF THE BILL:\n\nThis bill amends the Public Health Law Section 2164 and Education Law\nSection 3212 to include non-parents who possess lawful orders of custody\nin the definition of persons in parental relation who may make certain\nhealth and education decisions for minors or incapacitated persons in\ntheir care\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS::\n\nSection 2164 of this bill would amend the current Public Health Law 5\n2164 to include non-parents who possess lawful orders of custody as\npersons in parental relation of minors or incapacitated person, with\nlimited authority under the public health law, to consent to certain\nimmunizations.\n\nSection 3212 of this bill would amend the current Education Law 5 3212\nto include non-parents who have lawful orders of custody as persons in\nparental relation of minors or incapacitated person, with limited\nauthority under the education law, to be responsible for school attend-\nance and certain schooling decisions.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY::\n\n5/26/17 - Referred to Judiciary\n5/4/18 - Reference Changed to Health\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION::\n\nPublic Health Law Section 2164 and Education Law Section 3212 define who\nmay be a \"person in parental relation\". Such persons have limited powers\nregarding responsibility for schooling, school enrollment, and immuniza-\ntions of children in their care. The definitions do not include persons\npossessing lawful orders of custody from a court of competent jurisdic-\ntion. Since many non-parent caregivers have such orders and since\nalready Domestic Relations Law Section 74 and Family Court Act Section\n657 provide that guardians and legal custodians can enroll children in\nschool, there is a need to establish conformity of powers for legal\ncustodians in relation to certain health and schooling duties.\n\nAccording to the U. S. Census American Community Survey data, in New\nYork State, there are more than 130,000 grandparent \"kinship\" families.\nTogether with aunts, uncles and other family members, estimates are as\nmany as 200,000 kinship families are raising 300,000 children in New\nYork State. Kinship caregivers are defined as non-parents, including\ngrandparents, blood relatives, and close family friends who provide\nfulltime care and control of children in their household.\n\nWhile the Office of Court Administration does not maintain data on the\nnumber of non-parent caregivers awarded legal custody or guardianship,\nit .is commonly acknowledged that in many family.courts kinship caregiv-\ners pursuant to Article Six section 651 are independently awarded orders\nof legal custody. According to the Office for Children and Family\n.Services, it is also common practice that relatives are often given\norders of custody of children who are at risk of being removed from\ntheir parents by Child Protective Services pursuant to Family Court Act\n1017 (2) (a) (i), 1055 (a) or 1089 (b) .\n\nThe omission of persons possessing lawful orders of custody from the\npublic health law definition of person in parental relation who may be\nresponsible for consenting to the certain immunizations of minors does\nnot comport with the realities of kinship care where many legal custo-\ndians are responsible for routine health care and immunizations. By\nexpanding the law to include caregivers possessing lawful orders of\ncustody, tens of thousands of legal custodians will have the appropriate\nhealth and education responsibilities that comport with the realities of\ncaregiving.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS::\n\nNone to the State\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE::\n\nImmediately\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
